#{[17592186569946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569946 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efa5ab19-180d-5954-8556-a06af878d726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569973 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=-32y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "288339ac-4de2-5c41-bc2e-90c16e62e9fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569973 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(-8)y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-8.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570011 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2a/2a69b06fdf2997514ed4ba507461010725d0b453aaa56f0e0c87f901ee7fa466.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92645059-4fec-5363-9452-fc24032daa5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570011 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/7b/7b402346a77c6c52467fda2d7f488d123f9551c18cb06cac96765bda3c4b99d7.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570020 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-2)^{2}=24x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b967fd96-33d4-527f-b0fa-13d15459432d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570020 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}=4(6)(x-0).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=6.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=0$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570037 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=8y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7d54a09-b694-5b89-8845-bc7803f20047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570037 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(2)y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=2.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570040 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570040 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570040 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570040 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/20/20b0a99d75653873c8175ff1a65092d23674d9931a1d44848fe939cdb63896af.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3bdf4a3-1557-5260-88ea-6727a0119093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570040 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of a downward-opening parabola is the highest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/e6/e65f778d5ac36e8dd2cadf3f34effb50f13c7c74f05f491edb7f659f205883f1.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
